In the above equation,  
 \begin{equation\}  
 2\pi F\_\Delta mT\_M = 2\pi \frac{F\_\Delta}{R\_M} m = 2\pi F\_0 m  
 \end{equation\}  
 where the $F\_0$ is defined as the normalized Carrier Frequency Offset (nCFO): CFO normalized by the symbol rate.  
 \begin{equation\}  
 F\_0 = \frac{F\_\Delta}{R\_M}  
 \end{equation\}  
 This normalization is very important as we saw in the last subsection. The resulting expression takes the form  
 \begin{equation\}  
 \begin{aligned}  
 z\_I(mT\_M) = a\_I[m] \cos 2\pi F\_0m – a\_Q[m]\sin 2\pi F\_0m \\  
 z\_Q(mT\_M) = a\_I[m] \sin 2\pi F\_0m + a\_Q[m]\cos 2\pi F\_0m  
 \end{aligned}  
 \end{equation\}  
 In polar form, this expression can be written as  
 \begin{equation\}  
 \begin{aligned}  
 |z(mT\_M)| &= \sqrt{a\_I^2[m] + a\_Q^2[m]} \\ [...] \begin{align}  
 r(t) &= v\_I(t) \sqrt{2} \cos \Big[2\pi (F\_C+F\_\Delta) t + \theta\_\Delta\Big] – v\_Q(t) \sqrt{2}\sin \Big[ 2\pi (F\_C+F\_\Delta) t + \theta\_\Delta \Big]\nonumber\\  
 &= v\_I(t) \sqrt{2} \cos \Big[2\pi F\_Ct + 2\pi F\_\Delta t + \theta\_\Delta\Big] – \nonumber \\ &\hspace{2in}v\_Q(t) \sqrt{2}\sin \Big[ 2\pi F\_C t+ 2\pi F\_\Delta t + \theta\_\Delta\Big]\label{eqRealWorldQAMFreqOffset}  
 \end{align}  
